    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 71 tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors), including the new `Cp59Tests` that asserts the `application/problem+json` content type and `$.status`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change: **all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) now return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body** with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nFour of the escalation handlers already emitted RFC7807 problem details via the existing `ProblemDetails` builder (`Validation`, `Forbidden`, `DataIntegrityViolation`, `General`). The eight rejection handlers, however, still returned an ad-hoc `{\"errors\": [...]}` JSON map. I converted each of those to build a `ProblemDetail` via the existing `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)` helper and send it as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` \u2014 which Spring's return-value handling (used by the OfficeFloor starter) serializes as `application/problem+json`:\n\n- **400** \u2014 `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`, `MissingOwnerFields`\n- **409** \u2014 `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n- **429** \u2014 `DailyLimit`\n\nHTTP status codes are unchanged; `ProblemDetail.forStatus(...)` sets the `status` member to match, `type` defaults to `about:blank`, `title` is the exception's simple name, and each handler supplies a human-readable `detail` (the `MissingOwnerFields` one lists the offending fields). No test asserted the old `errors` body, so nothing else needed adjusting.",
